A||||6||Deer_Slayer||Prices at Junkyards||||8||
Z||000000||Deer_Slayer||01-18-2000||03:27 PM||||Some of the prices at junkyards can be outrageous.  Earlier, I called a local yard for a price of a single glass sailpannel to replace the cracked plexiglass ones on my 86.  Their price was $52 for only the one.  Heck, I can order a pair from Kick Hill Farm for $60.  Trying to make money I reckon.||||reg||8
Z||000001||mshill||01-18-2000||03:34 PM||||Just within the last month I noticed that the local "Pick'n'Pull" prices went up as well.  Two months ago I got a clutch master cylinder for $7.  Last week it was $10.  Still a great deal but for as much time I spend searching junk yards, I could have got a job at McDonalds and paid for all of my parts.  But, then again, that's part of the hobby that I love.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||01-18-2000||05:05 PM||||Sometimes, if you do alot of "shopping" at a single boneyard, you can get better deals.  I usually go to only one (although I check at a few others) and I've been able to get some better deals than if I only shopped there once in a while.  Only problem now is that the "used" Fiero market seems to have dried up around here.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Tom88gt||01-19-2000||12:55 PM||||The junkyard is in the buisness to make money.  They can't give it away.  Typical minimum price for any part is probably $5-10.  Otherwise, it isn't worth their time even operate the cash register.  <P>There are a lot of nickle & dime parts for the Fiero that you probably can get new at the parts store or from a Fiero catalog for about the same price.<P>Basically, I don't even consider a junkyard as a source unless its the only source or the savings approaches say.....50%, min $10 (I'm guessing here because I don't have a written in stone rule).  Saving $5 bucks on a part isn't worth it if the part is already 12+ years old.<P>Bottom line...how bad do you need it?  And do they know?||||reg||6
Z||000004||DRH||01-19-2000||01:31 PM||||Doesn't hurt to get to know them. The price on a lot of stuff (especially on older cars) is set on the spot and can vary from one person to the next.<P>Tom brings up a good point about the $5-10 minimum. When it comes to little stuff, the more you get at once the better. Something like a headlight switch might be 5 or 10. A whole armload of misc. switches, trim pieces, etc. might be only 20 or 25. This is most effective at the u-pull-it places. Just throw it on the counter and say "how much do I owe you?" They usually don't try to value each item. They just lump it all together and are usually pretty reasonable.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||mshill||01-19-2000||01:47 PM||||Something else I found is that a part may be $10 but you can get a whole lot of stuff that is attached to it for free.  Case in point, I needed a clutch slave and a slave mounting bracket for my 86GT.  The Fiero store wanted something like $85 for the bracket alone.  I got the slave with the bracket attached for $8 (the price of the slave).  Even if I had to buy a slave rebuild kit ( I didn't ), I saved a ton of money.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||01-19-2000||05:34 PM||||I agree.  I went to a boneyard looking for a set of power mirror heads.  The yard wanted $75 for the pair (not bad when you consider that Kick Hill wants that much for each one).  The doors on the donor car were in decent shape with power everything and the yard made me a deal of $150 for both doors complete.  Now I have lots of spare parts for a fraction of what they would have cost me if I had purchased them separately.||||reg||1
